# {{ project_name }}

A VPC for services, generated from the `network` template: {{ az_count }} public subnets{% if nat_mode != 'none' %}, {{ az_count }} private subnets and {% if nat_mode == 'single' %}one NAT gateway{% else %}one NAT gateway per zone{% endif %}{% else %}, no private subnets and no NAT gateway{% endif %}, in `{{ cidr }}`. It is built on `terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws` 6.7.3, pinned to the commit of its tag.

## Why it is a separate project

A network outlives the services in it, and several services share it. In its own Terraform root and state:

- destroying or replacing a service never touches the network;
- a service plan never shows network changes;
- you apply this project rarely, after reading its plan.

Services do not read this project's state. They read the SSM parameters it publishes:

| Parameter | Value |
| --- | --- |
| `/landform/network/{{ project_name }}-{{ environment }}/vpc-id` | the VPC ID |
| `/landform/network/{{ project_name }}-{{ environment }}/public-subnet-ids` | comma-separated public subnet IDs |
| `/landform/network/{{ project_name }}-{{ environment }}/private-subnet-ids` | comma-separated private subnet IDs; only when `nat_mode` is not `none` |
| `/landform/network/{{ project_name }}-{{ environment }}/nat-mode` | `none`, `single` or `per-az` |

To use the network, give `ecs-web` or `alb-ec2` the answer `network_name = "{{ project_name }}-{{ environment }}"`.

## What it contains

| Part | Setting | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Zones | the first {{ az_count }} zones that offer `{{ zone_instance_type }}` | Not every zone offers every instance type |
| Public subnets | /24 each, public IPv4 on launch | Instances there reach the internet without NAT |
| Default security group | no rules | Nothing uses it by accident |
| Flow logs | CloudWatch Logs, {{ log_retention_days }} days, 10-minute aggregation | A record of accepted and rejected traffic |
{% if nat_mode != 'none' %}| NAT | {{ nat_mode }} | Private subnets reach the internet; each NAT gateway costs about 32 USD a month plus data |
{% endif %}
## Prerequisites

Terraform ≥ 1.10 (tested with 1.14.4), and network access for `terraform init`: it downloads the AWS provider and the VPC module. {% if backend == 's3' %}The state bucket `{{ state_bucket }}` must exist before `init`; state locking uses the S3 lock file.{% else %}State is local (`infra/terraform.tfstate`): fine for a sandbox, not for a team. Switch to an S3 backend before sharing.{% endif %}

## Test

```bash
terraform -chdir=infra init -backend=false
terraform -chdir=infra test
```

## Deploy

The first `init` writes the provider versions and their hashes to `infra/.terraform.lock.hcl`. Commit `infra/.terraform.lock.hcl`: the checks then run `init` with `-lockfile=readonly`, so a provider cannot change without a reviewed commit. If CI runs on another OS, add its hashes first: `terraform -chdir=infra providers lock -platform=linux_amd64 -platform=darwin_arm64 -platform=windows_amd64`.

```bash
terraform -chdir=infra init
terraform -chdir=infra plan -var-file=envs/{{ environment }}.tfvars -out=tfplan
terraform -chdir=infra show tfplan
terraform -chdir=infra apply tfplan
```

After the first apply, copy the `availability_zones` output into `zones` in `infra/envs/{{ environment }}.tfvars`. Without the pin, a change in which zones offer `{{ zone_instance_type }}` would move the subnets, and replace everything in them.

## Remove

Remove every service that uses the network first. AWS refuses to delete a subnet that still has network interfaces in it.

```bash
terraform -chdir=infra plan -destroy -var-file=envs/{{ environment }}.tfvars -out=destroy.tfplan
terraform -chdir=infra apply destroy.tfplan
```
